WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Every day, we see so many things: the trees, the sky, and the screen in front of us.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.100
It all seems effortless.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:15.120
But as you recognize these things, your visual system
is already turning the light entering your eyes into

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.480
neural signals, and groups of neurons in your brain
are working to make sense of that information.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.420
The electrical signals produced by this brain activity
are very faint, but we have ways to measure them.

6
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.955
An EEG cap, for example, can pick up tiny changes in voltage at the scalp.

7
00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:39.815
And hidden in those faint, noisy recordings, there
may be clues about which image someone just saw.

8
00:00:40.615 --> 00:00:45.555
Studying these responses can help us understand
how the brain processes visual information.

9
00:00:46.475 --> 00:00:51.515
It can also help us explore ways to reconstruct
what a person has seen from their brain activity.

10
00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:57.255
It's easy to imagine that different pictures might
produce different patterns of brain activity.

11
00:00:58.055 --> 00:00:59.695
So let's turn the problem around.

12
00:01:00.535 --> 00:01:02.678
Suppose I give you an EEG recording.

13
00:01:03.558 --> 00:01:05.778
Could you work out which picture this person saw?

14
00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:09.423
That's the idea behind EEG-to-image retrieval.

15
00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:16.060
In a typical setup, we give the model an EEG recording
and a gallery of two hundred candidate images.

16
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.760
Its job is to find the right one.

17
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.899
But here's a problem: EEG and images are two very different kinds of data.

18
00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:27.439
How is a model supposed to compare them?

19
00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:36.191
One common approach is to use two encoders, one for EEG and
another for images, and map their features into the same space.

20
00:01:36.951 --> 00:01:46.313
By training on many EEG–image pairs, the model learns to match EEG signals
with the right pictures, even if those pictures were never seen during training.

21
00:01:47.153 --> 00:01:51.793
When we have plenty of training data from the
same person, this can work surprisingly well.

22
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:57.992
In a zero-shot setting on THINGS-EEG2, with
two hundred candidate images that were not

23
00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:03.932
used in training, some within-subject methods reach
roughly eighty-five to ninety-one percent accuracy.

24
00:02:04.892 --> 00:02:06.692
That sounds pretty good already, doesn't it?

25
00:02:07.172 --> 00:02:11.536
But when we put that EEG cap on someone new, things get much harder.

26
00:02:11.536 --> 00:02:17.703
The model hasn't seen any labeled EEG from this
person and has only been trained on other subjects.

27
00:02:18.543 --> 00:02:23.703
In our benchmark, even the strongest baseline
reaches only about thirty-six percent accuracy.

28
00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:29.835
Of course, we could collect labeled EEG data
to calibrate the model for every new person.

29
00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:36.435
But that takes extra time and effort, and we'd have
to do it over and over again, for each new user.

30
00:02:37.055 --> 00:02:38.575
So what's going on?

31
00:02:39.195 --> 00:02:44.515
Why can the model read signals from a familiar
person so well, yet struggle with someone new?

32
00:02:45.275 --> 00:02:51.976
Your first thought might be: well, different people have
different brains, so their EEG responses can differ too.

33
00:02:52.746 --> 00:02:58.126
So we could show different people the same
pictures and compare their raw EEG waveforms.

34
00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:06.426
However, those waveforms are affected by all sorts of things:
recording conditions, background brain activity, and noise.

35
00:03:07.266 --> 00:03:11.706
Even if two signals look different, it's still hard to tell what that difference means.

36
00:03:12.666 --> 00:03:14.406
So let's try another approach.

37
00:03:15.166 --> 00:03:19.232
We look at the relationships between the EEG features for different pictures.

38
00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:24.352
For one person, how similar are their responses to a cup and a car?

39
00:03:25.072 --> 00:03:28.092
And is that relationship still there when we move to someone else?

40
00:03:28.952 --> 00:03:34.122
To compare the EEG features for the cup and the car, we use cosine similarity.

41
00:03:35.182 --> 00:03:42.222
It measures how closely two feature vectors point in the same
direction: the more similar their directions, the higher the score.

42
00:03:43.242 --> 00:03:45.022
Now suppose we have ten pictures.

43
00:03:45.022 --> 00:03:50.282
We calculate the similarity for every pair and arrange the scores in a matrix.

44
00:03:51.242 --> 00:03:55.722
The cup–car score, for example, goes where the cup's row meets the car's column.

45
00:03:56.522 --> 00:04:00.942
This gives us a representational similarity matrix, or RSM.

46
00:04:01.922 --> 00:04:07.041
It summarizes the relationships between these
pictures' EEG features within one person.

47
00:04:08.041 --> 00:04:13.701
And now we can ask: if different people see
the same pictures, will their RSMs look alike?

48
00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:20.461
Let's put different people's RSMs side by side, with
the same image order and the same color scale.

49
00:04:21.241 --> 00:04:21.841
Hmm.

50
00:04:22.421 --> 00:04:24.481
They look surprisingly similar, don't they?

51
00:04:25.062 --> 00:04:32.282
To put a number on that, we compare the similarity values at
corresponding positions in the matrices and calculate their correlation.

52
00:04:33.342 --> 00:04:37.842
Across target and source subjects, the average is about zero point six nine.

53
00:04:38.702 --> 00:04:39.602
That's quite high.

54
00:04:40.462 --> 00:04:46.467
It suggests that the EEG features for these pictures
form roughly similar structures within each person.

55
00:04:47.147 --> 00:04:50.107
Think of them as constellations with similar shapes.

56
00:04:50.727 --> 00:04:52.767
But that leaves us with a puzzle.

57
00:04:53.287 --> 00:04:58.447
If the relationships are so similar, why is cross-subject retrieval still so poor?

58
00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:02.827
Imagine we're exploring a galaxy with a map of five stars.

59
00:05:03.487 --> 00:05:08.647
We know where the stars are, and we've learned which
direction to steer our spaceship to reach each one.

60
00:05:09.407 --> 00:05:12.847
Now we want to use that map to navigate another galaxy.

61
00:05:13.627 --> 00:05:17.627
If the stars form the same pattern, we might expect the same directions to work.

62
00:05:18.247 --> 00:05:19.447
But what if they don't?

63
00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:22.107
What if we keep heading toward the wrong star?

64
00:05:23.107 --> 00:05:27.527
Here's the catch: the same shape can still face a different direction!

65
00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:30.927
In this example, the whole constellation has turned.

66
00:05:31.647 --> 00:05:37.427
The relationships between the stars are unchanged, but
our old directions no longer take us to the right place.

67
00:05:38.184 --> 00:05:42.180
Now, let's see if we can spot something like this in real EEG features.

68
00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.300
Here we have one source subject and one target subject.

69
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.260
We take their features for the same pictures and
project them onto a shared two-dimensional plane.

70
00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.700
There it is: a clear difference in orientation.

71
00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:00.760
Could this help explain why cross-subject retrieval works so poorly?

72
00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.460
Naturally, you'd ask: can we fix that shift?

73
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.620
Why not just rotate the two shapes until they line up?

74
00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.580
Well, there's a catch.

75
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:15.039
In our earlier analysis, we knew which EEG recording came from which picture.

76
00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:19.099
In actual use, we don't know those pairings for the new subject.

77
00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:27.827
So we don't know which points should line up, or how to turn the shape
so that EEG features for the same image end up in the same place.

78
00:06:28.967 --> 00:06:30.107
And there's another problem.

79
00:06:31.207 --> 00:06:32.947
Training involves several source subjects.

80
00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:37.107
Even if we knew the pairings, which source subject should we choose?

81
00:06:37.927 --> 00:06:46.468
Finally, in a real deployment, privacy requirements or storage limits
may mean that we can't even access the original source EEG recordings.

82
00:06:47.648 --> 00:06:49.568
So SCORE takes a different approach.

83
00:06:50.348 --> 00:06:54.814
We try to align the new subject's EEG features with the image feature space.

84
00:06:55.474 --> 00:06:58.314
But what do we mean by the image feature space?

85
00:06:59.074 --> 00:07:00.934
Remember the two encoders from earlier?

86
00:07:01.894 --> 00:07:05.324
The EEG encoder turns brain responses into feature vectors.

87
00:07:06.244 --> 00:07:09.902
We pictured those vectors as stars, making an EEG constellation.

88
00:07:11.062 --> 00:07:13.282
Now, let's move to the image side.

89
00:07:14.102 --> 00:07:18.942
Feed the candidate pictures through the image
encoder, and each one gets a feature vector too.

90
00:07:19.742 --> 00:07:23.002
Plot those features as points, and we get another constellation.

91
00:07:24.142 --> 00:07:25.882
This time, it's an image constellation.

92
00:07:27.122 --> 00:07:34.168
We can use this image constellation as a common reference,
bringing matching EEG and image features into similar positions.

93
00:07:35.428 --> 00:07:36.508
Why choose this reference?

94
00:07:36.888 --> 00:07:38.208
There are a few reasons.

95
00:07:39.428 --> 00:07:42.294
First, our final task is EEG-to-image retrieval.

96
00:07:43.394 --> 00:07:47.643
Aligning EEG features with image features works directly toward that goal.

97
00:07:48.663 --> 00:07:52.903
Second, for a given retrieval task, we have one shared candidate gallery.

98
00:07:53.563 --> 00:07:56.963
So we don't have to choose which source subject should define the reference.

99
00:07:57.723 --> 00:08:04.678
Third, during training, we already teach source subjects'
EEG features to align with their matching image features.

100
00:08:04.678 --> 00:08:10.918
The training images may differ from the test
gallery, but both use the same image feature space.

101
00:08:11.718 --> 00:08:18.035
Bringing a new subject's EEG into that space brings it
toward the same reference the model learned during training.

102
00:08:19.035 --> 00:08:22.035
And finally, the gallery is available at test time.

103
00:08:22.275 --> 00:08:27.125
So we can still use it even when the original source EEG recordings are unavailable.

104
00:08:27.985 --> 00:08:34.323
We call this process coordinate recovery: bringing
EEG features into the image coordinate system.

105
00:08:35.063 --> 00:08:39.023
And that's the idea behind SCORE: Subject Coordinate Recovery.

106
00:08:39.743 --> 00:08:43.143
Now, let's look at how the full SCORE framework works.

107
00:08:43.883 --> 00:08:46.403
There are two stages: training and testing.

108
00:08:47.143 --> 00:08:48.123
We'll start with training.

109
00:08:49.183 --> 00:08:55.100
We start with the same basic idea: bring EEG
features closer to their matching image features.

110
00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.800
But SCORE adds two new components.

111
00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.840
The first is Multi-Positive Alignment.

112
00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:05.220
During training, different people may have seen the same picture.

113
00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:10.628
Their EEG responses might look different, but they all correspond to that picture.

114
00:09:11.388 --> 00:09:20.060
So instead of treating just one EEG response as a positive match, we
treat responses from all source subjects who saw that picture as positives.

115
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.980
We bring them closer to the same image feature, rather than pushing them apart.

116
00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.420
This encourages the model to learn what those subjects have in common.

117
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:36.320
From the coordinate-system perspective, this also helps align
different source subjects with the same image coordinates.

118
00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:38.400
But is that enough?

119
00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:39.700
Not necessarily.

120
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:48.357
Even if the source subjects are well aligned during training, a brand-new
person's EEG features might still end up in a different orientation.

121
00:09:49.037 --> 00:09:53.197
So we introduce a second mechanism: Simulated Recovery.

122
00:09:54.277 --> 00:09:59.297
In each training batch, we choose one source
subject to play the role of someone new.

123
00:10:00.077 --> 00:10:04.402
First, we temporarily hide the EEG–image pairings for that subject.

124
00:10:05.302 --> 00:10:13.804
Then, the recovery procedure sees only the EEG features and the candidate
image features, and tries to figure out how their coordinates should line up.

125
00:10:13.804 --> 00:10:21.103
After recovery, we reveal the hidden pairings and check
whether the recovered EEG features retrieve the correct images.

126
00:10:22.043 --> 00:10:26.488
That gives us a recovery loss, which we use to train the EEG encoder.

127
00:10:27.248 --> 00:10:30.888
This lets the model practice working with recovered features during training.

128
00:10:31.788 --> 00:10:35.228
We'll leave the details of coordinate recovery until the testing stage.

129
00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:39.528
Together, these two components give us SCORE's training objective.

130
00:10:40.308 --> 00:10:48.948
Multi-positive alignment replaces the standard pairwise contrastive loss,
and we add the recovery loss with a weight to control its contribution.

131
00:10:49.948 --> 00:10:53.588
And remember: throughout training, we use only the source subjects.

132
00:10:54.348 --> 00:10:56.948
We don't have any data from the real target subject.

133
00:10:57.908 --> 00:11:01.108
Now training is complete, and the encoders are frozen.

134
00:11:02.088 --> 00:11:07.569
All that's available is the trained model, the
new user's unlabeled EEG, and the test gallery.

135
00:11:07.569 --> 00:11:12.764
There are no image labels for this person, and no original source EEG recordings.

136
00:11:13.704 --> 00:11:15.684
Let's move on to the testing stage.

137
00:11:16.424 --> 00:11:23.829
We'll call the EEG features used for retrieval the queries,
Q, and the candidate image features the gallery, G.

138
00:11:24.589 --> 00:11:27.529
So how can we turn Q toward G?

139
00:11:28.409 --> 00:11:29.709
There are three main steps.

140
00:11:30.709 --> 00:11:32.249
First, moment matching.

141
00:11:33.029 --> 00:11:37.229
Before rotating anything, we align the statistics of Q and G.

142
00:11:37.889 --> 00:11:44.775
For each feature dimension, we shift and rescale the EEG
features to match the gallery's mean and standard deviation.

143
00:11:45.775 --> 00:11:50.775
This makes their centers and scales more comparable,
preparing them for the alignment that follows.

144
00:11:51.695 --> 00:11:53.575
Second, landmark selection.

145
00:11:54.515 --> 00:12:00.995
Before we can turn Q toward G, we need some matched
reference points to tell us how their orientations differ.

146
00:12:01.755 --> 00:12:03.715
We call these reference pairs landmarks.

147
00:12:04.715 --> 00:12:08.415
To see why they help, let's look at a simple two-dimensional example.

148
00:12:09.215 --> 00:12:12.315
We have two identical shapes, facing different directions.

149
00:12:13.115 --> 00:12:15.555
Suppose we know one correct pair of points.

150
00:12:16.035 --> 00:12:21.115
We can pin those points together, but the rest
of the shape can still turn around that pin.

151
00:12:22.115 --> 00:12:23.435
What if we fix two pairs?

152
00:12:24.255 --> 00:12:28.575
That fixes the line between them, but the drawing could still flip across that line.

153
00:12:29.395 --> 00:12:30.195
And three pairs?

154
00:12:30.935 --> 00:12:31.575
There we go!

155
00:12:32.115 --> 00:12:34.995
With these three pairs, we can line up the two shapes.

156
00:12:35.875 --> 00:12:38.295
These matched reference pairs are our landmarks.

157
00:12:39.315 --> 00:12:41.675
But we don't have the new subject's labels.

158
00:12:42.395 --> 00:12:46.033
We don't know which EEG recording corresponds to which image.

159
00:12:46.033 --> 00:12:48.873
So where do our landmarks come from?

160
00:12:49.653 --> 00:12:50.893
Remember the model we trained?

161
00:12:51.633 --> 00:12:56.653
Its predictions for a new person may be imperfect, but they still contain useful clues.

162
00:12:57.513 --> 00:13:01.326
We can use those predictions to look for promising EEG–image pairs.

163
00:13:02.366 --> 00:13:06.266
A natural idea is to choose the pairs with the highest similarity scores.

164
00:13:07.086 --> 00:13:09.326
Those seem like the model's most confident matches.

165
00:13:10.166 --> 00:13:14.326
But in this setting, a high score doesn't always mean a reliable match.

166
00:13:14.326 --> 00:13:20.306
In a high-dimensional space, some points become
near neighbors of unusually many queries.

167
00:13:21.166 --> 00:13:22.146
That's hubness.

168
00:13:22.886 --> 00:13:29.145
An image like this can score highly for many different
EEG queries, even when it isn't the correct match.

169
00:13:30.025 --> 00:13:36.892
So before trusting a match, two questions matter:
is this image attracting too many EEG queries?

170
00:13:37.672 --> 00:13:42.018
And does this EEG query give high scores to lots of other images?

171
00:13:42.018 --> 00:13:44.678
Both can affect landmark selection.

172
00:13:45.818 --> 00:13:52.678
Fortunately, cross-domain similarity local scaling,
or CSLS, offers a way to reduce this effect.

173
00:13:53.118 --> 00:14:00.395
CSLS starts with twice a pair's cosine
similarity, then subtracts two local averages: the

174
00:14:00.395 --> 00:14:06.554
EEG query's similarity to nearby images, and
the image's similarity to nearby EEG queries.

175
00:14:07.234 --> 00:14:12.414
A hub tends to have a high neighborhood average, so it receives a larger penalty.

176
00:14:13.314 --> 00:14:16.414
That helps reduce its advantage when selecting landmark pairs.

177
00:14:17.494 --> 00:14:24.381
With these corrected scores, SCORE selects a pair only
when the EEG query and image are each other's first choice.

178
00:14:25.381 --> 00:14:29.241
These pairs become landmarks, helping make the selection more reliable.

179
00:14:30.261 --> 00:14:35.041
However, because of the distribution shift, some landmarks may still be unreliable.

180
00:14:35.041 --> 00:14:38.121
So SCORE also assigns weights.

181
00:14:39.061 --> 00:14:42.941
The more confident the model is, the more that pair contributes to the rotation.

182
00:14:44.121 --> 00:14:46.561
Now the landmarks and their weights are ready.

183
00:14:47.301 --> 00:14:48.801
It's time to recover the coordinates.

184
00:14:49.801 --> 00:14:51.701
Third, coordinate recovery.

185
00:14:53.041 --> 00:14:55.901
First, let's briefly introduce a rotation matrix.

186
00:14:56.901 --> 00:15:01.081
In two dimensions, it takes this form, where theta is the angle of rotation.

187
00:15:02.241 --> 00:15:06.861
Multiplying a point's coordinates by this matrix rotates the point by that angle.

188
00:15:07.601 --> 00:15:13.861
Besides rotation, SCORE also allows reflection, so it uses an orthogonal matrix, R.

189
00:15:14.821 --> 00:15:21.401
Since the landmarks have weights, SCORE first calculates
their weighted centers, then centers the two sets again.

190
00:15:22.461 --> 00:15:27.273
Here, X contains the EEG landmarks, and Y contains their image partners.

191
00:15:28.273 --> 00:15:32.413
Subtracting their weighted centers gives X sub c and Y sub c.

192
00:15:33.153 --> 00:15:35.733
The matrix W contains the landmark weights.

193
00:15:36.773 --> 00:15:39.953
With these definitions, the fitting objective looks like this.

194
00:15:40.853 --> 00:15:51.175
Multiply the centered EEG landmarks by R, measure their squared distances from
the corresponding image landmarks, then weight those distances and add them up.

195
00:15:51.175 --> 00:15:56.975
The goal is to find an orthogonal R that makes this total as small as possible.

196
00:15:57.835 --> 00:16:04.055
However, the features are high-dimensional, and a limited
set of reference pairs may leave some directions uncertain.

197
00:16:05.215 --> 00:16:07.635
So SCORE adds a regularization term.

198
00:16:08.555 --> 00:16:13.195
If there isn't enough information to support a change in a direction, the preference is

199
00:16:13.195 --> 00:16:18.175
to stay close to the identity matrix: in other
words, to leave that direction unchanged.

200
00:16:19.115 --> 00:16:26.515
That gives the final objective: the landmark fitting error,
plus a penalty for moving away from the identity matrix.

201
00:16:27.375 --> 00:16:29.755
Lambda controls the strength of that penalty.

202
00:16:30.695 --> 00:16:32.215
Now, let's solve it.

203
00:16:32.995 --> 00:16:36.815
First, expand the squared fitting error into these three terms.

204
00:16:37.755 --> 00:16:41.375
The trace notation here simply adds up a matrix's diagonal entries.

205
00:16:42.175 --> 00:16:43.535
Look at the first term.

206
00:16:43.535 --> 00:16:46.915
R is orthogonal, so it preserves lengths.

207
00:16:47.715 --> 00:16:54.175
Moving the factors around inside the trace brings R
and R transpose together; their product is the identity.

208
00:16:55.075 --> 00:16:57.175
This term no longer depends on R.

209
00:16:58.215 --> 00:17:00.335
The last term doesn't depend on R either.

210
00:17:00.989 --> 00:17:03.549
Next, expand the regularization term.

211
00:17:04.249 --> 00:17:10.449
Again, R transpose times R is the identity,
leaving a constant and this term involving R.

212
00:17:11.269 --> 00:17:15.649
Since those constants don't affect which R
gives the minimum, they can be removed.

213
00:17:16.809 --> 00:17:20.549
Combine the two remaining terms, and the expression becomes much simpler.

214
00:17:21.549 --> 00:17:26.289
Minimizing this negative quantity is equivalent to maximizing the trace shown here.

215
00:17:26.889 --> 00:17:33.309
Let's call this matrix M: the weighted landmark
cross-product, plus lambda times the identity.

216
00:17:34.309 --> 00:17:39.669
Apply SVD directly to M to obtain U, Sigma, and V transpose.

217
00:17:40.589 --> 00:17:43.989
Then the optimal R is U times V transpose.

218
00:17:44.869 --> 00:17:47.589
With this matrix, coordinate recovery is straightforward.

219
00:17:48.709 --> 00:17:57.677
To transform a moment-matched EEG query, subtract the EEG
landmark center, multiply by R, and add the image landmark center.

220
00:17:58.497 --> 00:18:00.670
That gives the recovered EEG features.

221
00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:06.350
The same operation applies to every query,
including those that weren't selected as landmarks.

222
00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:12.030
Now, back to the original question: which image did this person see?

223
00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:18.330
SCORE recalculates CSLS in the recovered
space, including the neighborhood averages.

224
00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:23.646
The highest-scoring image becomes the retrieval result for each EEG query.

225
00:18:24.706 --> 00:18:26.166
That completes the SCORE method.

226
00:18:26.886 --> 00:18:28.866
So, how well does it work in practice?

227
00:18:29.846 --> 00:18:34.601
Let's start with THINGS-EEG2, one of the best-known benchmarks in this field.

228
00:18:35.701 --> 00:18:43.241
The strongest baseline, CSLS without coordinate recovery,
reaches thirty-five point seven eight percent Top-1 accuracy.

229
00:18:44.241 --> 00:18:47.281
SCORE reaches fifty-three point two three percent.

230
00:18:47.281 --> 00:18:51.161
That's a gain of seventeen point four five percentage points.

231
00:18:52.101 --> 00:19:00.001
And looking at the subjects individually, SCORE improves
Top-1 accuracy over CSLS for all ten held-out subjects.

232
00:19:01.061 --> 00:19:08.121
On another dataset, Alljoined-1.6M, SCORE also
achieves the best results among the methods compared.

233
00:19:09.201 --> 00:19:14.361
Top-1 accuracy rises from eight point nine
three percent for the strongest baseline to

234
00:19:14.361 --> 00:19:19.381
twelve point zero one percent, a gain of three point zero eight percentage points.

235
00:19:20.161 --> 00:19:23.282
Let's look at one example from THINGS-EEG2.

236
00:19:23.862 --> 00:19:25.502
A person saw a cruise ship.

237
00:19:26.362 --> 00:19:30.462
With the standard SAMGA model, the correct image was ranked thirty-fourth.

238
00:19:30.942 --> 00:19:33.462
SCORE moved it all the way up to third.

239
00:19:34.302 --> 00:19:39.722
Together with the results across subjects and datasets,
this gives a picture of consistent improvements.

240
00:19:40.782 --> 00:19:42.862
Other experimental results are in the paper.

241
00:19:43.742 --> 00:19:48.942
Here, let's look at just one more interesting experiment: transfer to new concepts.

242
00:19:50.082 --> 00:19:57.812
So far, the full gallery has been used to estimate a recovery map,
which then transforms the EEG features toward that gallery.

243
00:19:58.192 --> 00:19:59.612
But what about a harder setting?

244
00:20:00.212 --> 00:20:03.832
Suppose only half the gallery is available for estimating the map.

245
00:20:04.532 --> 00:20:07.552
Can that same map still improve retrieval for the other half?

246
00:20:08.252 --> 00:20:12.432
Back in the galaxy example, suppose only part of the constellation is visible.

247
00:20:13.392 --> 00:20:16.452
Can a rotation fitted to that part also work for the rest?

248
00:20:17.372 --> 00:20:22.912
To test this, the test concepts are split into two groups of one hundred: A and B.

249
00:20:23.872 --> 00:20:30.732
Only group A supplies the information for estimating the
statistics, selecting landmarks, and fitting the recovery map.

250
00:20:31.512 --> 00:20:36.252
Those statistics and the map are then applied
to group B, without fitting them again.

251
00:20:37.172 --> 00:20:40.372
Retrieval is evaluated against B's one hundred images.

252
00:20:41.332 --> 00:20:42.212
Here are the results.

253
00:20:43.192 --> 00:20:50.160
The improvement remains: with CSLS ranking,
Top-1 accuracy on group B rises from fifty point four

254
00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:56.512
one percent without coordinate recovery to
sixty-four point one six percent with the frozen map.

255
00:20:57.092 --> 00:21:03.812
This suggests that the recovered coordinate relationship
can generalize to new concepts, supporting the idea that

256
00:21:03.812 --> 00:21:09.912
it captures a shared subject-to-image relationship
rather than just fitting the concepts used to estimate it.

257
00:21:10.492 --> 00:21:19.248
So, our diagnosis points to a difference in coordinate orientation as
one reason cross-subject EEG-to-image retrieval performs poorly.

258
00:21:20.188 --> 00:21:24.148
To address it, SCORE uses the image feature space as a shared reference.

259
00:21:24.928 --> 00:21:33.268
Training prepares the encoder for recovery; at test time, the new
subject's coordinates are corrected without labeled EEG–image pairs.

260
00:21:34.068 --> 00:21:35.828
That's the idea behind our work.

261
00:21:36.448 --> 00:21:38.188
Of course, there's still work to do.

262
00:21:38.908 --> 00:21:44.614
The THINGS-EEG2 results rely on EEG
responses averaged across repeated presentations.

263
00:21:45.634 --> 00:21:50.218
Recovery also needs a batch of unlabeled EEG recordings from a new subject.

264
00:21:51.058 --> 00:21:54.898
Next, we'd like to make this work with fewer and noisier responses.

265
00:21:55.818 --> 00:22:00.938
And that brings us back to a simple question: before asking every new user for a

266
00:22:00.938 --> 00:22:07.199
large labeled EEG dataset, can we make better use
of the information already in their brain signals?
